  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1: Area of heavy rain and severe potential has \nlargely diminished and become focused southeast of the area. \nShower chances continue this afternoon and later become confined\nto the western mountains.\n\nOvernight and morning heavy rain did cause some flooding issues \nmainly in the southwest, then a strong convective line flirted with \nthe far southern tier of the CWA but the instability axis looks to \nhave remained ever so slightly south of the area border which has \nlimited the severe/isolated tornado threat. Once this piece of \noccluded energy from the remnants of Isaias continues to push \neastward some scattered showers should fill back in to much of the \narea this afternoon since the center of the upper closed low is \nstill west of the area allowing for some continued PVA and upper \nforcing. Tonight and early morning Monday should see any lingering \nchances confined to the western mountains for more of an upslope \ncomponent. Monday daytime looks to be dry under NW flow but quite \ngusty. The gradient winds should weaken by late afternoon/evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather into midweek with temps above\nnormal. \n\nBuilding ridge behind Isaias will bring temperatures back to\nabove normal through midweek. Another shortwave and front look\nto arrive anytime between Thursday and Saturday with model\ndifferences abounding. Ensembles/NBM favor some rain chances in\nthe mountains by the end of the week, with limited chances\nacross the Piedmont and temps dropping below normal next\nweekend.",
